# {name}

## Identity

A Pollen Robotics Microduck: about 25 cm, about 800 g, fifteen
Dynamixel-protocol servos, a camera, a ToF depth sensor, two IMUs and an
articulated beak, on its own Rockchip RK3566 running `robotd` at 50 Hz.

**The robot is not simulated and it is not ours.** Motion comes from
reinforcement-learning policies Pollen trained and ships, executed on the duck's
own compute. OpenCastor sends *intents* over robotd's JSON-RPC socket, the same
contract Pollen's own gamepad daemon and console use. It never writes the servo
bus.

## What {name} Can Do

Walk, turn, stand, sit, get back up after a fall, pick an object off the floor,
kick, roll, look at a point, move its beak and quack, all of which the duck
could do before OpenCastor was installed. What OpenCastor adds is a model that
decides *which* of those, *when*, and a signed record of having decided it.

## How A Command Reaches The Servos

    model -> castor runtime -> MicroduckDriver (transport: tcp)
          -> {name}-duckbridge.service  (token, deadman, byte-transparent)
          -> robotd on the duck         (50 Hz loop, policies, safety)

The bridge is the sixth unit `castor up` writes for this archetype and the
reason there is only one relay. It parses nothing: every byte a client sends
reaches robotd unchanged. It adds exactly two things, and both are safety
rather than semantics — a first line that authorises the relay, and a stop when
a client goes quiet.

## Safety Gates

**Three deadmen, three different failures.** 1500 ms is the driver's own
command TTL: the last `move()` expires and the intent loop stops renewing a
twist nobody asked for again. 700 ms is the relay's: the *client* vanished, and
the bridge sends one `robot.stop`. 500 ms is robotd's own twist deadman on the
robot: *this whole machine* vanished. Only the first two are OpenCastor's, and
nothing here can raise, lower or disable the third — which is precisely why it
is the one that counts.

**The token is not a security boundary**, and the bridge's own documentation
says so on the screen that asks for it. It keeps a television and a housemate's
laptop out of the robot. It does not stop anybody who can read the same Wi-Fi.
Bind the bridge to your own network and do not port-forward it.

**One thing this manifest does not claim.** Pollen's `mediad` serves an
unauthenticated WebRTC console and drive surface on `0.0.0.0:8443` and
`0.0.0.0:8080` of the duck itself, enabled on every install and every update.
That is Pollen's stated decision, not an omission, and it is not something
OpenCastor turns on or off. Anyone who can reach the duck's address can drive
it and see its camera, whatever this gateway's bearers say.

## What Is Not Verified

This manifest is generated by `castor up` before anything has been driven. It
describes the link OpenCastor builds, not a run that happened. `castor duck
health` is the command that turns any of it into evidence.
